PART A – PERSISTENCE DESIGN & PSEUDOCODE
TASK A1 – DESIGN A FILE FORMAT (7 Marks)
Problem: Transport Office Bus-Pass Records

The university transport office stores approximately 8,000 bus-pass records every year. Each record contains seven fields.
A1.1 – Represent the Record (2 Marks)
(a) Delimited Text File Design

Use the pipe symbol (|) as the delimiter.

Record format:
PassID|RegisterNo|Name|RouteNo|BoardingStop|ValidityDate|AmountPaid


Example:
BP101|23CSE001|Ravi Kumar|12|Tambaram|2027-06-30|5000
BP102|23ECE015|Priya|15|Guindy|2027-06-30|4500
BP103|23IT020|Arun Kumar|12|Chromepet|2027-06-30|5000


Field description:
Field	Data type	Example
Pass ID	String	BP101
Register Number	String	23CSE001
Name	String	Ravi Kumar
Route Number	Integer	12
Boarding Stop	String	Tambaram
Validity Date	Date (YYYY-MM-DD)	2027-06-30
Amount Paid	Decimal	5000.00

Handling names containing the delimiter:

If a name contains the pipe symbol, escape it using a backslash.

Example:
BP104|23CSE030|Ravi\|Kumar|12|Tambaram|2027-06-30|5000


A backslash inside a field must also be escaped as \\. The reading program must decode these escape sequences correctly.

Advantages:
- Easy to read and understand.
- Easy to edit using a text editor.
- Requires less storage for ordinary records.
- Suitable for a small transport office application.
(b) Binary File Design using DataOutputStream

Use DataOutputStream to store records in binary format.
Field	Binary representation
Pass ID	writeUTF()
Register Number	writeUTF()
Name	writeUTF()
Route Number	writeInt()
Boarding Stop	writeUTF()
Validity Date	writeUTF()
Amount Paid	writeDouble()

Field order:
Pass ID
Register Number
Name
Route Number
Boarding Stop
Validity Date
Amount Paid


Example Java writing statements:
DataOutputStream out = new DataOutputStream(
    new FileOutputStream("passes.dat", true));

out.writeUTF("BP101");
out.writeUTF("23CSE001");
out.writeUTF("Ravi Kumar");
out.writeInt(12);
out.writeUTF("Tambaram");
out.writeUTF("2027-06-30");
out.writeDouble(5000.00);

out.close();


Advantages:
- Stores data in compact binary form.
- Supports different data types.
- Faster to read and write structured values in many situations.
- Prevents ordinary users from easily modifying the data using a text editor.
Disadvantage: Binary files are not directly human-readable, and reading must follow exactly the same field order and data types used during writing.
A1.2 – Design the Operations (3 Marks)
We need three operations:
1. Add a new bus pass.
2. Find a bus pass using Pass ID.
3. List all bus passes belonging to a particular route.
Stream classes used:
Stream class	Purpose
FileReader	Reads character data from a text file
BufferedReader	Reads text efficiently, line by line
FileWriter	Writes character data to a text file
BufferedWriter	Writes text efficiently using buffering
FileInputStream	Reads raw bytes, useful for binary files
FileOutputStream	Writes raw bytes, useful for binary files

For the delimited text design, BufferedReader and BufferedWriter are preferred because the application processes complete records one line at a time.
Algorithm 1 – Add a New Pass
Pseudocode
ALGORITHM AddNewPass

START

INPUT PassID, RegisterNo, Name
INPUT RouteNo, BoardingStop
INPUT ValidityDate, AmountPaid

IF any required field is empty THEN
    OUTPUT "Invalid input"
    STOP
END IF

OPEN file using BufferedReader

FOR each existing record
    IF record PassID equals new PassID THEN
        OUTPUT "Pass already exists"
        CLOSE file
        STOP
    END IF
END FOR

CLOSE file

ENCODE special characters in text fields

OPEN file using BufferedWriter in append mode

WRITE new pass record
WRITE newline

CLOSE file

OUTPUT "Pass added successfully"

END


Explanation: The application checks whether the Pass ID already exists. If it does not exist, the new record is appended to the text file.
Algorithm 2 – Find a Pass by Pass ID
Pseudocode
ALGORITHM FindPass

START

INPUT SearchPassID

IF file does not exist THEN
    OUTPUT "Pass file not found"
    STOP
END IF

OPEN file using BufferedReader

Found ← FALSE

WHILE another line is available

    READ one line

    IF line is valid THEN

        DECODE record

        IF record PassID equals SearchPassID THEN

            OUTPUT complete pass details

            Found ← TRUE
            EXIT LOOP

        END IF

    ELSE

        OUTPUT "Damaged record skipped"

    END IF

END WHILE

CLOSE file

IF Found = FALSE THEN
    OUTPUT "Pass not found"
END IF

END


Explanation: The application reads each line and compares its Pass ID with the requested ID. When a match is found, it displays the complete record.
Algorithm 3 – List Passes by Route Number
Pseudocode
ALGORITHM ListPassesByRoute

START

INPUT SearchRouteNo

IF file does not exist THEN
    OUTPUT "Pass file not found"
    STOP
END IF

OPEN file using BufferedReader

Found ← FALSE

WHILE another line is available

    READ one line

    IF line is valid THEN

        DECODE record

        IF record RouteNo = SearchRouteNo THEN

            OUTPUT complete pass details

            Found ← TRUE

        END IF

    ELSE

        OUTPUT "Damaged record skipped"

    END IF

END WHILE

CLOSE file

IF Found = FALSE THEN
    OUTPUT "No passes found for this route"
END IF

END


Explanation: The application reads all records and displays only the records whose route number matches the user's input.
A1.3 – Design for Damaged Data (2 Marks)
A file may become damaged because of power failure, unexpected program termination or incorrect manual editing.

1. Damaged Line
- Check whether each line contains all seven fields.
- Validate the route number and amount.
- Validate the date format.
- Check whether the Pass ID is present.
- Skip invalid records and write their line numbers to an error log.
- Continue processing the remaining records.
Example:
BP105|23CSE010|Ravi|12


This record is incomplete because it contains only four fields.

Expected message:
Damaged record found.
Record skipped.


2. Missing File
- Check whether the file exists before reading.
- If the file is missing during a search or listing operation, display an error message.
- When adding the first record, create the file if it does not exist.
- Do not treat a missing file as proof that a searched pass does not exist.
Example:
File file = new File("passes.txt");

if (!file.exists()) {
    System.out.println("Pass file not found.");
}


3. Incomplete Write Due to Power Failure

Use the following protection methods:
1. Write each record as a complete line.
2. Flush buffered data and close the file safely.
3. Include a checksum or record-length validation to detect incomplete records.
4. Write updates to a temporary file before replacing the original file.
5. Use an atomic file replacement operation where the file system supports it.
6. Keep a backup of the original file.
For an append-based design, a partially written final line should be detected and rejected rather than treated as a valid record.
Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
Question: What if the records are stored using RandomAccessFile with fixed-length records?
1. RandomAccessFile Design
A RandomAccessFile allows the program to move directly to a particular position in the file.

Assume each record has a fixed size of 200 bytes.

The position of record i is:
\[
Position = i \times RecordSize
\]

Example:
RandomAccessFile file =
    new RandomAccessFile("passes.dat", "rw");

int recordSize = 200;
int recordIndex = 5;

file.seek((long) recordIndex * recordSize);


This moves the file pointer directly to record number 5.
2. Time Complexity Comparison
Operation	Delimited text file	Fixed-length RandomAccessFile
Add record	O(1) append, ignoring validation scan	O(1) if an unused slot is known
Find by ID	O(n) scan	O(n) scan without an index
Read known record position	O(n) sequential traversal in general	O(1) seek
List by route	O(n) scan	O(n) scan without a route index

Important evaluation: Random access gives O(1) access when the record's index or position is already known. Finding a record by Pass ID is not automatically O(1). The application must scan records or maintain an additional Pass ID-to-position index.
3. Space Wasted by Padding
Fixed-length records reserve the same amount of space for every field, even when the actual data is shorter.

Example:
Field	Allocated size	Actual data
Pass ID	20 bytes	5 bytes
Register Number	20 bytes	9 bytes
Name	50 bytes	10 bytes
Route Number	4 bytes	4 bytes
Boarding Stop	50 bytes	10 bytes
Validity Date	10 bytes	10 bytes
Amount Paid	8 bytes	8 bytes
Total	162 bytes	56 bytes

Wasted space per record:
\[
162-56=106\text{ bytes}
\]

For 8,000 records:
\[
106\times8000=848,000\text{ bytes}
\]

Approximately 828 KiB is wasted in this example, excluding any additional record headers or indexing information.
4. Final Comparison
Feature	Text File	RandomAccessFile
Readability	Human-readable	Binary / fixed format
Finding by ID	Sequential search	Sequential search unless indexed
Known-position access	Not direct	O(1) seek
Storage	Variable-length	Fixed-length
Padding waste	No fixed-field padding	Possible
Updating records	Usually rewrite or append	Can update at a known position
Implementation	Simple	More complex

Final Decision: For 8,000 bus-pass records, a delimited text file is a simple and suitable choice because it is easy to read, maintain and edit. If the application requires frequent direct access to records and their positions are known, RandomAccessFile is useful. If fast lookup by Pass ID is important, an additional index should be maintained.

